          <div>
            <div class="chapter-title" style="text-align:left;font-size:1.5rem;margin-bottom:0.3rem;">House Mévouimeur</div>
            <div class="chapter-subtitle">Arset's Premier House of Art &amp; Culture</div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>House Mévouimeur is one of Arset's oldest and most complicated noble families. The Mévouimeurs are not the most powerful house in Arset but they control what culture looks like in a kingdom that did not think it cared about culture until the Mévouimeurs made it care. They have used that control with the patience and precision of a family that has been playing a very long game and is not in any hurry to finish it. They are also, depending on who you ask and how well you know them, either the most dangerous family in Arset or the most misunderstood one. The Mévouimeurs have never particularly minded the question. They commissioned a beautiful piece of art about it once and hung it somewhere everyone would see it.</p>

        <div class="section-head">Historical Origins</div>

        <p>The family emerged during a period when Arset was consolidating its identity as a kingdom, moving beyond the immediate business of survival into the longer business of deciding what kind of place it intended to be. The first Mévouimeurs of record were builders, commissioned to design public buildings in Arset's growing capital at a time when the crown wanted structures that looked like they belonged to a kingdom rather than a collection of fortified settlements.</p>

        <p>The Mévouimeurs built what was asked of them and then built more, not because they were commissioned to but because they saw what the spaces could be and could not leave them as less. This habit of exceeding the brief, of doing more than was asked because less was insufficient, became the defining characteristic of everything the family produced and eventually of the family itself.</p>

        <p>From architecture they expanded into painting, sculpture, music, literature, and quietly and carefully into banking. The banking began as small loans to artists; they patronized a sculptor who needed materials, a composer who needed time, a writer who needed not to starve while finishing something important. Then larger loans to minor nobles who needed funds and had nowhere else to turn. Then larger still to houses that were struggling and grateful. The Mévouimeurs never called themselves bankers. They called themselves patrons. The distinction mattered to their reputation and not at all to the ledgers.</p>

        <p>By the time Arset's court fully understood what the Mévouimeurs had built it was already built. And all of them had Mévouimeur art on their walls, Mévouimeur music at their celebrations, and Mévouimeur names attached to the cultural moments that defined their family histories.</p>

        <div class="section-head">The Arts</div>

        <div class="section-head" style="font-size:0.9rem;border-bottom:none;margin-top:0.5rem;margin-bottom:0.3rem;">Painting &amp; Sculpture</div>

        <p>The Mévouimeurs cultivated artists across three centuries with the patience and precision of people who understood that great art does not appear fully formed but has to be grown. They found talent young, funded development, attached their name to the results, and ensured that the most significant works ended up in locations where the most significant people would see them.</p>

        <div class="margin-note">The family's private collection is the subject of considerable speculation — works never publicly displayed, portraits of people whose identities the family has never confirmed, pieces that scholars have spent careers trying to see and been politely refused.</div>

        <p>The family's private collection is the subject of considerable speculation, works that have never been publicly displayed, portraits of people whose identities the family has never confirmed, pieces that scholars have spent careers trying to see and been politely refused. There are persistent rumors that certain works in the collection depict scenes and subjects that would be politically explosive if they became public, private moments of public figures, documented indiscretions, proof of things that powerful people have spent significant resources denying.</p>

        <p>Whether these works exist as described or whether the Mévouimeurs simply allow the rumor to exist because a family that might have evidence of your secrets is almost as useful as a family that definitely does, this is the kind of question the Mévouimeurs do not answer.</p>

        <div class="section-head" style="font-size:0.9rem;border-bottom:none;margin-top:0.5rem;margin-bottom:0.3rem;">Music &amp; Literature</div>

        <p>The Mévouimeurs have funded more poets, composers, and writers than any institution in Arset's history. They have also, quietly and consistently, shaped what those artists produced. Not crudely; the Mévouimeurs are too sophisticated for obvious propaganda. They simply made certain kinds of work easier to produce and certain kinds of work harder to find an audience for. A poet who wrote things the family found useful found patrons and audiences. A poet who wrote things the family found inconvenient found that opportunities had mysteriously dried up.</p>

        <p>This has never been proven. It has been suspected by everyone who has worked in Arset's artistic community for three centuries. The Mévouimeurs have always expressed genuine hurt at the suggestion.</p>

        <div class="section-head">The Banking &amp; Political Power</div>

        <div class="section-head" style="font-size:0.9rem;border-bottom:none;margin-top:0.5rem;margin-bottom:0.3rem;">The Loans</div>

        <p>The Mévouimeur banking operation began quietly and became enormous. They lent to artists first, then to nobles, then to the crown itself during periods of financial strain. They never demanded obvious political concessions in return, they simply remembered. A house that has lent you money during a crisis and asked for nothing in return is a house you feel obligated to. The Mévouimeurs collected obligations the way other houses collected land and found them considerably more flexible. Several noble houses in Arset owe their current status entirely to Mévouimeur loans made two or three generations ago.</p>

        <div class="section-head" style="font-size:0.9rem;border-bottom:none;margin-top:0.5rem;margin-bottom:0.3rem;">The Crown</div>

        <p>The Mévouimeurs have had a complicated relationship with Arset's crown for three centuries, close enough to be useful, careful enough never to be threatening, and powerful enough that the crown has always found it easier to work with them than against them. Their position as Arset's premier cultural house has given them something no military house has, a legitimate reason to maintain relationships with Avelyn's court. The Mévouimeurs have been crossing the six hundred miles of water between Arset and Avelyn for generations, attending Avelyn cultural events, maintaining relationships with Avelyn's own artistic families, and building a network that spans both kingdoms in a way that no purely political house could justify or sustain. This cross-kingdom network is the Mévouimeurs' most significant political asset and their most carefully guarded one.</p>

        <div class="section-head" style="font-size:0.9rem;border-bottom:none;margin-top:0.5rem;margin-bottom:0.3rem;">The Church</div>

        <p>The Mévouimeurs have funded religious buildings, commissioned sacred art, and provided financial support to clergy across both Arset and Avelyn for three centuries. They have also had members of the family in significant church positions at almost every point in their history. Whether these clergy members entered religious life out of genuine faith or family strategy is a question the Mévouimeurs find offensive and that the historical record finds genuinely difficult to answer.</p>

        <p>There are rumors that the family has used its church connections to access information that ought to be protected, not directly, not crudely, but in the way that a family with three centuries of practice at gathering information finds ways to be adjacent to it without being directly responsible for the gathering. These rumors have never been substantiated. The family's clergy have always been regarded as genuinely pious. And the Mévouimeurs have always seemed to know things that are difficult to explain.</p>

        <div class="section-head" style="font-size:0.9rem;border-bottom:none;margin-top:0.5rem;margin-bottom:0.3rem;">The Marriages</div>

        <p>The Mévouimeurs have arranged marriages with the strategic precision of a family that understands marriage as a political instrument first and a human relationship at some distance second. They have also demonstrated over three centuries a pattern that their critics find troubling; spouses who came from families the Mévouimeurs needed access to tended to die young, leaving the Mévouimeur partner in control of the deceased's family assets and connections. The deaths were always natural. The timing was always convenient. The pattern was always explained as tragedy.</p>

